            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>There are many areas that are showing signs of air leakage, the areas are listed below:



Around the edges of the attic hatch.



Around the door frame and edges of the doors.



The back door to the deck is very leaky.



The floor header in the basement, and around top/bottom plates.



The edge of the ceiling where the wall meets the celining truss is very leaky. There is also mispaced insulation around these areas.



The electrical receptacles are showing signs of air leakage.







</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>The mail walls are in need of an upgrade. Adding insulation on the exterior of the home would be the easiest way to fix this. You will improve the overall insualtion value of the wall while decreasing air flow through the building envelope. This will also lower the amount of oil needed to heat the home.</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>The ceiling will eventually need an upgrade at some point down the road. It is not an area that should be assessed right away. There is a rea sonable amount of insulation in the ceiling. Around the edge of the ceiling is where you are losing your insulation value. The heel height of the truss is quite small and therefore you cannot get the same amount of insulation in this area as you could in the middle. In the future, I would recommened you spray foam around the edges of the ceiling and around any electrical or ventilation penetration. And then adding a sufficicent amount of insulation on top of that (R-40 at minimum). The spray foam has double the r-value of blown in cellulose or fiberglass batt, which makes for ideal insulation in areas where space is an issue.</ceilings>
                <ceilingsCathedral />
                <foundation>The foundation is the area where you are losing most of your heat. The easiet solution to improving this would be to add R-20 of rigid board insulation to the exterior. Doing this will also improve the air tightiness of the home.</foundation>
                <windows>Most of the windows are new and do not need to be replaced for a long time. The back door to the deck should be replaced. It does not keep heat in as well as other windows in the house and it is also very leaky. Improving this will address both of the issues stated above as well as improving the comfort level in the dining room/kitchen area.</windows>
                <doors>The doors need some work done around the frame. There is quite a bit of air leakage. This can be addressed by adding new weather stripping around the doors. In the future you may want to look at replacing the doors, The windows in each door is only double glazed. The metal insulator in each also allows for conductive heat loss through the frame of the window.</doors>
                <VentilationSystem>If you do decide to make your home more efficient and air tight, it is a good idea to possibly take a look at installing a HRV (heat recovery ventilation system). This will allow the home to be properly ventilated, and will also improve the air quality in the home. Currently the home is getting heated air frome the furnace as well as fresh air that is leaking into the home through the building envelope. If you decide to add insualtion to the exterior and air seal in the home, you will be cutting off the fresh air supply that enters through cracks and small holes. By air sealing and installing a HRV, you will be able to properly ventilate the home and avoid any health issues such as mold/mildew.</VentilationSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>The furnace is getting older  and may be due for an upgrade in the coming years. There are a couple options here:



Electric Furnace:



You can use existing ductwork. Will not have to worry about back drafting as no exhaust is needed for this and any chimney can be blocked off. Electricity is 100% efficient. 



Electric Baseboards:



They are 100% efficicent and easy to have installed. 



Condensing Oil Furnace; 



You can use existing ductwork. You will  not need a chimney as this unit uses PVC pipe as an exhasut becuase the temperature of the exhaust is so low. The combustion air will be sealed off from the interior of the home and can avoid back drafting. 



Make sure that this furnace has a high quality heat exchanger and high static burner to aviod any soot build up in the exhaust and to avoid corrosion of the heat exchanger.



These Units are 90-95% efficient.



Condensing Propane Furnace:



You can use existing ductwork. You will  not need a chimney as this unit uses PVC pipe as an exhasut becuase the temperature of the exhaust is so low. The combustion air will be sealed off from the interior of the home and can avoid back drafting. 



You don't have to worry about corrosion or soot build up like you would with a condensing oil furnace (though this should not happen) as propane is easier to extract heat from than oil. You also will not have to worry about any chances of an oil spill and issues with insurance on oil tanks.



These units are 95% efficient.



Ways to Upgrade existing Furnace:



Have a brushless DC Motor Installed. This can reduce the average furnace electrical consumption by 70% or more.

Seal all joints in ductwork with a water based duct mastic (sealant) or a high temperature tape. The tape tends to degrade and permit air leakage over time compared to the sealant.

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>Great roof for a solar array!</Renewables>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
